n't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, South Bend has set <strong class=\"sig\">29 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 75 that chance alone would give, and <strong>74 record highs</strong>, close to the 75 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (60). South Bend's all-time high is 109\u00b0F, on July 24, 1934.","t_chg":"South Bend's nights warmed over the long run. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 124 complete years: South Bend's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.18\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.25), about +2.4\u00b0F since 1894. South Bend's average high shows no clear change: +0.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.03 to +0.12) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows show no clear change and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.45\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.90). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 6 of 12 months. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months; July and August fell.","t_rain":"South Bend has gotten wetter","p_rain":"South Bend's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.27 to +0.83), about +7.1 inches since 1895. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 39.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (55.61 in); the driest, 1917 (22.17 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}